A **discriminated union** is a union of object types that share a literal 'tag' field. TypeScript narrows the type when you check the tag, so each branch only sees its own fields.

```ts
type Result<T> =
  | { ok: true; data: T }
  | { ok: false; error: { code: string; message: string } };

function show(r: Result<Order>) {
  if (r.ok) return r.data.total;   // data is available here
  return r.error.message;          // only error here
}
```

**Why it is better than optional fields** (`{ data?: T; error?: E }`): impossible states (both or neither) cannot be represented, and the compiler forces you to handle both.

**Exhaustiveness:** for unions with many tags (`status: 'idle' | 'loading' | 'success' | 'error'`), a `switch` with a `default` that assigns to `never` makes the build fail when someone adds a new state and forgets to handle it.

**At the boundary:** types disappear at runtime, so validate API responses with Zod (or similar) and infer the type from the schema.

Sliding window only works for non-negative numbers, so use **prefix sums with a hash map**.

Idea: if `prefix[j] - prefix[i] = target`, the subarray `(i, j]` sums to target. Store the **first** index where each prefix sum appears (first index gives the longest subarray).

```csharp
int Longest(int[] a, int target) {
    var first = new Dictionary<long, int> { [0] = -1 };
    long sum = 0; int best = 0;
    for (int j = 0; j < a.Length; j++) {
        sum += a[j];
        if (first.TryGetValue(sum - target, out int i)) best = Math.Max(best, j - i);
        first.TryAdd(sum, j);           // keep the earliest index only
    }
    return best;
}
```

**Complexity:** O(n) time, O(n) space.

**Edge cases to mention:** empty array (0), whole array is the answer (the `[0] = -1` seed handles it), overflow (use `long`), target 0.

**Variants:** count subarrays with sum k (store counts instead of first index), longest with equal 0s and 1s (map 0 to -1, target 0).

**Merge:** sort by start time. Walk through; if the current interval starts at or before the last merged end, extend the end to `max(end, current.end)`; otherwise start a new interval. O(n log n) for the sort, O(n) after.

```csharp
var s = intervals.OrderBy(x => x[0]).ToList();
var res = new List<int[]>();
foreach (var cur in s) {
    if (res.Count > 0 && cur[0] <= res[^1][1]) res[^1][1] = Math.Max(res[^1][1], cur[1]);
    else res.Add(new[] { cur[0], cur[1] });
}
```

**Insert into a sorted, non-overlapping list (no re-sort):** three phases in one pass: copy intervals that end before the new one starts; merge all that overlap into the new interval (min start, max end); copy the rest. O(n).

**Edge cases:** touching intervals ([1,2],[2,3]): clarify whether they merge; empty list; new interval before all or after all.

**Related:** minimum meeting rooms = sort starts and ends separately, or a min-heap of end times.
